<%--
Sso_Buttons

One "Continue with ..." button per federated sign-in provider this install has switched on.

    <Sso_Buttons />

On a settings page, where the visitor is already signed in and is CONNECTING an account
rather than signing in with one, pass the link intent instead:

    $intent="link"

THEY ARE ANCHORS, NOT AJAX, and that is the whole reason this component has almost nothing in
it. A sign-in ceremony is a browser NAVIGATION: the button leaves this site for the provider,
and the provider navigates back to /_sso/<key>/callback - a top-level GET that carries the
SameSite=Lax session cookie holding the parked state. An Ajax call could not leave, and a
fetch of the callback would arrive without the cookie the ceremony depends on.

IT RENDERS NOTHING WHEN NOTHING IS ENABLED. Not an error, not an empty box, not a heading:
window.rsxapp.sso is absent entirely when no provider is live, so a page that drops this in
unconditionally simply shows nothing. A login page that also wants an "or" divider tests
Rsx_Sso::is_enabled() (PHP) or Rsx_Sso.is_enabled() (JS) for the divider itself.

IT IS LAYOUT-NEUTRAL. No card, no heading, no outer spacing - the host owns the box, and the
buttons fill whatever width they are given. That is what lets one component serve a narrow
login card and a wide settings section without either one fighting it.

THE DATA COMES FROM THE SERVER, not from a fetch: Rsx_Sso.enabled_providers() reads
window.rsxapp.sso, which the bundle exports from Rsx_Sso::enabled_providers(). Every entry is
{key, label, begin_url, icon_svg} and is public by contract - no client id, no secret, nothing
that is not already visible in the authorize URL the button leads to.

BRAND MARKS ARE THE PROVIDERS'. The shipped SVGs are the official marks, inlined because the
framework serves no static assets. Every provider publishes brand guidelines governing the
mark, the wording beside it and the button's minimum size, and an application that restyles
these buttons is responsible for staying inside them - Google's in particular are a review
requirement, not advice. The default wording here ("Continue with X") is the phrasing all five
permit.

WHY THESE ARE OUTLINE BUTTONS, against the house rule that buttons are filled. A filled
button would have to be filled with SOMETHING, and every colour available is either the
application's primary - which asserts that signing in with Google is this page's primary
action - or one of the providers' own brand colours, which their guidelines govern and
several of them forbid. A neutral bordered button carrying the official mark is the one shape
all five permit, and it is what every provider's own reference implementation looks like.

Args:
  $intent  login (default) - sign in with the provider
           link            - connect the provider to the identity that is ALREADY signed in.
                             Refused by the server when nobody is signed in, rather than
                             quietly downgraded to a sign-in.

See: php artisan rsx:man sso
--%>
<Define:Sso_Buttons tag="div">
    <% for (const provider of this.data.providers) { %>
        <a class="Sso_Buttons__button btn btn-outline-secondary" href="<%= this.begin_url(provider) %>"
           data-provider="<%= provider.key %>" rel="nofollow">
            <% if (provider.icon_svg) { %>
                <span class="Sso_Buttons__icon"><%!= provider.icon_svg %></span>
            <% } %>
            <span class="Sso_Buttons__label">Continue with <%= provider.label %></span>
        </a>
    <% } %>
</Define:Sso_Buttons>
